WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:03.810
- [Narrator] No texts or emails
from your boss after hours,

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.240
meeting-free Fridays

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.490
and an app that kicks you out of chats.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:08.940
Some companies and governments,

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.160
such as Spain, Portugal and France

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.483
want employees to unplug from work.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.850
- I don't feel bad or
I don't have the urge

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.060
of respond, and if I'm
enjoying my social life,

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.910
if I'm with my family or friends.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:23.290
- [Narrator] The pandemic thrust the issue

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:24.390
of disconnecting from work

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.100
under a new light as millions

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.040
across the globe began working from home.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.760
In one poll conducted by
software company Buffer,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.970
nearly 1/3 of respondents said unplugging

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.630
from their duties after hours

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.080
was the hardest adjustment.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.480
- One implication is clear
from ongoing research.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.000
People need time to disconnect.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.290
- [Narrator] Amid a wave
of resignations in the US

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.180
with as many as 4.4
million people leaving jobs

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.040
in September, companies are looking

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.530
for solutions in order
to retain their workers.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.440
Goldman Sachs said it would enforce limits

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.960
on working hours after
some employees complained

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.090
they were working 95 hours per week.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.780
While this San Francisco startup

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.950
is testing digital tools
to make workers disconnect.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.880
- We'd rather solve those
problems than understand them.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.620
And the only way that we see to do that

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:07.960
is to start experimenting.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.860
- [Narrator] To find out what
these business policies do

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.400
for company productivity
and employee retention,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.340
we look to Europe where legislation

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.070
to give workers the right to disconnect

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.070
has been implemented in several countries.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.000
- I think the employees are more active,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.260
more proactive and more productive.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.880
- [Narrator] Although
technology helped employees

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.680
stay connected during the pandemic,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.340
for many, it also blurred the lines

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.460
between work life and home life.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.710
- If you have work on your mind,

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.710
you can't really be present in both places

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:33.900
at the same time.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.070
- [Narrator] That's Liuba Belkin,

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.730
a professor at Lehigh University

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.100
who has researched the
impact of receiving emails

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.030
from work out of hours
on employee well-being.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.810
- It not only hurts the non-work life

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.360
but it also might impede
your productivity as well

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.300
because you're not really
thinking about work either.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.600
You're not fully present for work.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.010
- [Narrator] But companies
that tell workers

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.000
to disconnect face a number of risks.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.590
Belkin's research argues that
if disconnection policies

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.320
are not implemented correctly,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.460
they could actually end
up having negative effects

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:03.500
on mental health.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.340
- Yes, workers have to
have a right to disconnect,

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.540
however, there are certain jobs

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.410
that prohibiting people to check email

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.610
actually might increase anxiety

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.070
because you would be
thinking what's going on?

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:17.500
What if there is an emergency

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.600
or something else is happening?

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.340
- [Narrator] In Europe
where Portugal's parliament

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.610
recently announced labor laws

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.780
to give workers the right not
to be contacted after hours,

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.780
companies have had mixed reactions,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.470
while some have complained that such rules

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.383
could hurt flexibility,
others have embraced them.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.900
This is the Spanish branch
of global insurer AXA,

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.780
which introduced disconnection policies

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.610
in a collective bargaining agreement

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.330
as the right to disconnect became law

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.773
in France and Spain in 2017 and 2018.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.860
The Spanish law calls on employers

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.430
to set up internal policies

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.480
that define how employees exercise

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.260
their right to disconnect.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.430
But how does that work in practice?

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.750
- We had room for improvement.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.170
So that's why we took the decision

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.290
to promote it even more,

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.910
to improve some behaviors,

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:06.530
to improve some habits

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.690
that are present in the Spanish culture.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.770
Maybe in the past, we tend
to have meetings later

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:13.833
in the evening.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.210
- [Narrator] We visited Nuria,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.620
an employee who is
mostly working from home.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.210
Her job is to provide training

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:21.570
to managers and workers

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.670
and she is required by company policy

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.040
to finish her meetings by 6 PM.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.210
At the end of her working day,

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:31.060
Nuria uses an app to log
how many hours she's worked.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.240
- So whenever I finish my working day,

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:35.280
what I usually do

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.280
is get in my phone, log into
an app called Tempo Mobile.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:43.740
Press the Salida, which is exit

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.900
and I just go out to enjoy my day.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.130
- [Narrator] After that,
she says she uses her time

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:52.383
to catch up with friends,
like her colleague Alberto.

00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:56.800
(Nuria speaking in foreign language)

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.750
- [Narrator] According to the
company's internal surveys,

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.590
employee loyalty and satisfaction

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:14.250
at AXA has improved significantly.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.110
- Legal disconnections
produce more efficiency,

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.950
productivity, and good relationships.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.280
- [Narrator] It's hard
to determine exactly

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.050
how these policies have
influenced business performance.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:27.670
Overall, AXA's parent company
had a challenging year in 2020

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.763
with shrinking sales during the pandemic.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.610
Legislation that requires
companies to refrain

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.770
from contacting employees after hours

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.940
has not been enacted in the US.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.340
- In the United States, the
culture's so much work oriented.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.730
I think it would be very tough battle

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.800
to pass any laws in the
United States like that.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.440
- [Narrator] State
lawmakers in New York City

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.010
proposed a Right to
Disconnect Bill in 2018.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.460
However, it was opposed
strongly by business leaders

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.510
during a public hearing in 2019.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:58.920
- We do not support the
Right to Disconnect Bill

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:00.750
due to concerns that this
would negatively impact

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.030
our member businesses,

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:04.444
particularly those that
are small businesses.

00:05:04.444 --> 00:05:05.707
- It's unwise to use New York City

00:05:05.707 --> 00:05:07.890
as a testing ground for
such a novel approach.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.140
- [Narrator] Some US businesses

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:11.100
have considered
introducing their own rules

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.630
to help workers to unplug.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.440
Loom, a tech startup developing

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.040
a video communication platform

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.357
has more than 170 employees.

00:05:18.357 --> 00:05:21.480
- People are working at
Loom all around the world.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.510
- [Narrator] Its co-founder
says to help employees

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:26.100
in different time zones
not to work after hours,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.440
the startup created a program

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.410
that can disconnect
people from group chats

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.440
in the communication platform Slack.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.640
- And it's a really simple thing.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.840
It just automates the flow
of kicking out of channels.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.090
- There's no hard data yet

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.390
but Loom says it is evaluating the impact

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:41.760
of the policies, which are meant

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.780
to help attract and retain talent

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.700
during a period of economic growth.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:48.360
- Employees in tech have more optionality

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.150
than they've ever had before.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.260
Keeping your employees happy and engaged

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.690
in this hiring market in technology

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.330
where people can work anywhere

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.330
and from anywhere, and workers in tech

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.090
are more in demand than ever

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.043
is just like good for business.

00:06:02.043 --> 00:06:04.626
(lively music)

